WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:17.340
Now, we didn’t want to start this channel out
with a video about tardigrades.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:23.500
We wanted to share at least a bit about
some of the other mind blowing organisms of

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:25.130
the microcosmos first.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:29.500
But we also knew it wasn’t going to be long
before we got to them.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:34.450
We are, it must be admitted, tardigrade enthusiasts.

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:39.680
And we’ve been excited to watch as a fair
number of people have joined us in that enthusiasm.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:45.980
However, of course, and we’re not mad about
this, the popular narratives about tardigrades

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:55.640
have, in their enthusiasm, gotten a few things less than correct, and also skipped over some of the most

00:00:55.640 --> 00:01:01.880
interesting facets of this celebrity of the
microcosmos.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:04.400
So...tardigrades.

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:12.110
They’re chubby and they have heads and legs and butts and they look, at first glance, somewhat

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:13.110
familiar.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:20.710
That’s why we sometimes call them water
bears or, even better in our opinion, moss piglets because

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:26.360
they are often in the aquatic environments
that cling to mosses.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:30.710
But they are also very unlike macro-animals.

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:33.670
For one thing, they have eight legs.

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:41.390
For another thing they are not closely related
to anything else on earth.

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:47.600
Though taxonomists are still fighting about this, the leading system places tardigrades inside

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:52.400
a clade called Tactopoda that includes two
groups.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:54.840
There's tardigrades and there's something called

00:01:54.840 --> 00:02:05.940
Euarthropoda. And that other clade includes basically every bug, insects, arachnids, even crustaceans

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:07.700
and trilobites.

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:17.310
The organism that branched away from tardigrades has literally millions of species as its ancestor.

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:22.660
But the branch that tardigrades sit on...just tardigrades.

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:26.420
Though there are over 1000 described tardigrade species

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:31.000
and there are likely many times more than that out there.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:38.520
But nonetheless, they do look a lot like bears with their claws and stumpy four pairs of legs.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:48.340
Tardigrades hover right on the edge of microscopic.

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:53.040
They range widely in size from 50 microns long to 1200 microns

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.020
but usually, they're under 500 microns in size.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:01.980
Tardigrades can be found in three major
aquatic environments.

00:03:01.989 --> 00:03:08.790
Some live in marine or brackish water, others in fresh waters like ponds, lakes, and rivers,

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:16.380
and then there are the terrestrial habitats but this isn't exactly what it sounds like.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:22.380
See, tardigrades do live on what we would
consider land, but they live in films of water

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:25.500
clinging to plants and dirt.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:33.590
This thin film, though nearly invisible to
us, can be a Tardigrade’s whole world.

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:39.819
But these films can easily dry up in the daytime...which
is part of why Tardigrades are so good at

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:46.880
surviving difficult situations, which of course we’ll be discussing shortly.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:57.760
They occupy almost every place on earth, from
the Arctic to the Antarctic, from soil to beach sand,

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:03.120
rivers, lakes, streams, on mosses, lichens,
algae and plants.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:09.629
We know of a couple of places where we will almost certainly find them waddling around in samples

00:04:09.629 --> 00:04:10.989
we take.

00:04:10.989 --> 00:04:15.920
And we will make a whole episode about that process but that’s for later.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:22.229
Although they are aquatic, tardigrades don’t swim, they kind of walk.

00:04:22.229 --> 00:04:28.040
In fresh water, they live among vegetation
and mainly feed on the cell contents of plants

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:34.320
and algae by piercing the cells with the two
stylets they have in their mouthpart but

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:40.240
some species eat single-celled organisms or even other micro animals including rotifers

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:43.040
and also other tardigrades

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:53.580
Some species of tardigrades have eyes, besides helping them look super cute, they also boggle

00:04:53.589 --> 00:04:58.830
the mind because they aren’t eyes.

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:05.490
To be an eye, you have to be an organ, and
to be an organ, you have to be composed of

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:08.800
tissues that are themselves composed of cells.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:17.220
But each tardigrade eye is, in fact, a single
photoreceptive cell.

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:19.760
That is wild.

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:25.560
On their whole body, sometimes containing tens of thousands of cells,

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:32.340
they have two cells, and those two cells, are there two little eyes

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:39.940
Tardigrades are also covered in a kind of
skin, or shell, called a cuticle.

00:05:39.949 --> 00:05:45.949
Coloration is produced by the pigmentation
of the cuticle and also the content of the digestive

00:05:45.949 --> 00:05:46.949
tract.

00:05:46.949 --> 00:05:52.499
Gray, bluish, yellow brown, reddish or brown
are common colors.

00:05:52.499 --> 00:05:58.620
This cuticle always stays the same size, and
so must be shed as the tardigrade grows.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:05.580
Some species of tardigrades lay their eggs
into this cuticle, which expands to hold them.

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:12.949
And then when the cuticle is shed, the eggs stay inside, giving them an extra layer of protection from

00:06:12.949 --> 00:06:14.639
roving predators.

00:06:14.639 --> 00:06:21.529
If those eggs manage to avoid being eaten, they will hatch into tiny, little tardigradelettes that,

00:06:21.529 --> 00:06:27.599
oddly enough, have the same number of cells that they will always have.

00:06:27.599 --> 00:06:32.949
But their cells, and thus their bodies, will
grow larger.

00:06:32.949 --> 00:06:39.580
Tardigrades are marveolously complex for such
tiny animals, they have digestive systems

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:43.189
and salivary glands, even small brains.

00:06:43.189 --> 00:06:47.219
The most complex have over 40,000 cells.

00:06:47.219 --> 00:06:53.770
And yes, when they’re done extracting nutrients
from their food, they have to get rid of it

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:55.220
just like the rest of us.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:04.500
Of course, tardigrades are best known for
their survival skills.

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:09.500
Many species have the ability to survive environmental extremes,

00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:15.260
such as temperatures as low as -272 degrees Celsius and as high as 150 degrees celsius.

00:07:15.260 --> 00:07:21.809
Extreme radiation.

00:07:21.809 --> 00:07:26.260
Extreme pressures as high as 6000 atmospheres.

00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:31.999
NASA sent a bunch of tardigrades
to outer space and brought them back alive

00:07:31.999 --> 00:07:40.100
after exposing the animals to the vacuum of
space and extreme radiation from the sun.

00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:46.460
A tardigrade may have a life-span of a couple
of weeks to more than 100 years.

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:52.500
A museum specimen of a dried moss that was in a herbarium for 120 years

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:56.240
yielded active tardigrades when soaked in water.

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:03.380
Tardigrades are able to survive these extremely hostile

00:08:03.389 --> 00:08:08.649
conditions by undergoing a process known as
cryptobiosis.

00:08:08.649 --> 00:08:18.129
In cryptobiosis, the metabolism of the tardigrades
can lower to less than 0.01% of normal and

00:08:18.129 --> 00:08:23.420
when conditions get better the tardigrades simply get back to life.

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:27.480
There are actually several of kinds of cryptobiosis.

00:08:27.499 --> 00:08:33.020
In Anhydrobiosis are dealing with a lack of water,

00:08:33.020 --> 00:08:38.280
and they curl up into a ball called a tun to being all dried up

00:08:38.600 --> 00:08:46.380
A glass-like protective sugar, trehalose is
synthesized and it replaces the water in the

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:48.200
cells of the tardigrades.

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:54.800
This sugar prevents crucial parts of the cells from getting damaged during water loss and

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:58.280
lets tardigrades to survive the dry period.

00:08:59.180 --> 00:09:03.520
Anoxibiosis occurs when there is insufficient
oxygen.

00:09:03.530 --> 00:09:07.580
The body of the tardigrade swells and becomes
rigid and turgid.

00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:12.330
There is no movement and the tardigrade looks
dead.

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:15.710
This sometimes happens in our prepared slides.

00:09:15.710 --> 00:09:22.220
So when it happens, we simply blow air into the slide from the side for around 10 seconds

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:29.950
and that is enough to increase the oxygen
concentration and “revive” the water bear.

00:09:29.950 --> 00:09:35.370
And there are some other types of cryptobiosis such as cryobiosis where tardigrades can withstand

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:42.400
extremely low temperatures and osmobiosis where they can withstand changes in osmotic pressure.

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:54.980
One of the misconceptions about tardigrades
is that they are immortal.

00:09:54.990 --> 00:10:00.760
They are resistant to these extreme conditions
when they are in tun state, but otherwise

00:10:00.760 --> 00:10:07.480
they are quite delicate animals and can be
hurt and killed easily.

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:14.250
Because of their small size, tardigrades are eaten by some eve bigger single-celled organisms.

00:10:14.250 --> 00:10:20.310
In this video, we managed to record a close encounter between tardigrade and a giant

00:10:20.310 --> 00:10:21.310
eukaryote.

00:10:21.310 --> 00:10:26.150
We don’t even know what this single-celled
organism here is, let us know if you think

00:10:26.150 --> 00:10:32.410
you can identify it, but it could easily eat
the tardigrade and digest it.

00:10:32.410 --> 00:10:37.920
But this lucky little one managed to escape and even damaged the cell of the eukaryote in the process

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:45.640
And in this clip we have a happy tardigrade
waddling on one of the most dangerous residents

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:48.860
of the ponds, a brown hydra.

00:10:48.860 --> 00:10:54.790
Hydra’s tentacles are armed with something
called nematocysts.

00:10:54.790 --> 00:11:02.140
These are like tiny harpoons that are fired upon contact to immobilize the hydra's prey.

00:11:02.140 --> 00:11:09.710
This water bear was either very lucky or too small to activate the nematocysts and so got away

00:11:09.710 --> 00:11:12.640
from a heartbreaking end.

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:20.060
Tardigrades are not extremophiles, they don’t
enjoy living in extreme conditions they are

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:22.690
just good at surviving them.

00:11:22.690 --> 00:11:27.080
In that way, maybe they are something like
us.

00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:33.540
We also sometimes live through situations
we thought would be impossible.

00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:41.070
But, all things being equal, both tardigrades
and us just want a nice place to live, plenty

00:11:41.070 --> 00:11:48.760
of yummy food, and whenever possible, to not be exposed to the vacuum of space.

00:11:48.760 --> 00:11:56.470
We are so happy that these friends share
their world with us, so often just outside

00:11:56.470 --> 00:12:02.150
of our notice, but successfully chomping their
way through the microcosmos for more than

00:12:02.150 --> 00:12:04.560
half a billion years.

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:08.920
And just in case you wanted to see it again...

00:12:08.920 --> 00:12:11.440
(laughs)

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:13.000
That's good.

00:12:13.220 --> 00:12:20.420
Thank you for coming on this journey with us through the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:12:20.420 --> 00:12:24.200
If you want to see more from our Master of Microscopes, James

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:27.640
check out Jam and Germs on Instagram.

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:31.740
And if you want to see more from us here at Journey to the Microcosmos,

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:35.420
I bet there's a subscribe button somewhere nearby.

00:12:35.540 --> 00:12:39.700
We really appreciate everybody's kind words in the comments.

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:43.660
